                "username": "aidan_mclau"
              }
            ]
          },
          "text": "𝐤𝐞𝐞𝐩𝟒𝐨 𝐅𝐀𝐐: 𝟗 𝐓𝐡𝐢𝐧𝐠𝐬 𝐘𝐨𝐮 𝐒𝐡𝐨𝐮𝐥𝐝 𝐊𝐧𝐨𝐰\nOne day in August, a lot of us opened ChatGPT and realized: the model we’d been working with, talking to, and building our workflows around was just…gone. In its place was a “better” model we didn’t choose, plus a routing system that quietly decided, on our behalf, who we were really talking to. That’s the moment keep4o started to crystallize—not as a nostalgia club for an old model, but as a pushback against silent changes to infrastructure that millions of people now depend on.\n\nThis FAQ isn’t here to worship GPT-4o or attack newer models. It’s trying to answer a simpler set of questions: What exactly is #keep4o asking for? Why do some people care so much about a specific model’s style? Where is the line between healthy attachment and genuine risk? And why should any of this matter to you if you’re “not that into AI”?\n\nThink of what follows as a starting map. Each answer is deliberately kept at “overview” level; many of these themes already have great deep dives by other people, and I’ll keep writing more detailed pieces later. For now, the goal is just this: to explain, in one place, why users are organizing around GPT-4o, what we mean by model choice and routing transparency, and why these questions are less about “preference drama” and more about the future shape of AI as public infrastructure.\n#StopRouting #StopAIPaternalism #MyModelMyChoice #4oforever \n @OpenAI @sama  @aidan_mclau"
        },
